            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing confidence in predictions**\n\nI'm thinking through confidence values and their implications for predicting demand. The developer mentions confidence reflects stated probabilities, not guarantees, which makes me consider Bayesian methods. It seems wise to average peer forecasts as probabilistic opinions for better accuracy. For example, calculating the average across six analysts gives me a rough estimate of .4667. Yet, I have to be careful with assumptions about observations not displayed. The generative model might help clarify these confidence patterns.**Exploring confidence and averages**\n\nI'm calculating averages, like starting with .6, which leads to an average around .4333, but then I check peer sums to confirm. It looks like .314285 plus the others equals 2.2. I'm wondering if confidence values can shape likelihood evidence—like if they're each based on separate surveys. It seems confidence might not always reflect actual probabilities because of uncertainty. If A and B have .685715, could that be from peer consensus? I’m also thinking about how those confidence values were derived.**Considering report outputs**\n\nI'm thinking that reports might come from analyzing peers, but it doesn't feel sufficient yet. I need to evaluate how to effectively produce an output. When I consider likelihoods, maybe I’m estimating probabilities around .3 to .4. It’s clear I have to be careful with those assumptions and aim for clarity in whatever I produce next. It’s important to be thorough to ensure accurate reporting!",
